You can generate a single integer x and ask whether $$$p[x] \ge m$$$ or $$$p[x] \le m$$$ for any $$$m$$$ ($$$1 \le m \le n$$$). In other words, each question contains one integer $$$x$$$ ($$$1 \le x \le n$$$) and one integer $$$m$$$ ($$$1 \le m \le n$$$), which you think is a guess in your opinion (you don't reveal $$$x$$$ to the grader).
If $$$p[x] = m$$$, then we get an answer equal to $$$-1$$$ or $$$1$$$, otherwise, we will receive the answer $$$0$$$ or $$$2$$$, but you shouldn't care about the difference between them.
Handle incorrect queries properly (e.g., if $$$x < 1$$$ or $$$x > n$$$ return -1; if $$$m < 1$$$ or $$$m > n$$$ return -2).
The interaction ends when you guess the correct permutation. To indicate that you know the answer, print "-2" in a separate line. After printing a query do not forget to output end of line and flush the output. Otherwise, you might get Idleness limit exceeded verdict.
To do this, use:
Hacks format
For hacks, use the following format:
In first line output single integer $$$n$$$ ($$$1 \leq n \leq 90$$$).
In next line output number $$$k$$$ ($$$\lceil{\log_2(n+1)}\rceil \leq k \leq n!$$$) — maximal number of queries required for valid interaction.
In next lines output all $$$k$$$ queries. Each query consists of two lines. First line contains single integer $$$x$$$ ($$$1 \leq x \leq n$$$), second line of query contains single integer $$$m$$$ ($$$1 \leq m \leq n$$$).
Do not forget that if your program receives an answer different from $$$-1$$$, $$$0$$$, $$$1$$$ or $$$2$$$, then you made an invalid query and should immediately exit (for example, by calling exit(0)). If your program detected that it made an invalid query, it should also exit.
Also, if at some point your program reads "$$-$$", then you printed an invalid query or read results of a query incorrectly.
Example
Interaction
Let's say that secret permutation is $$${p}=\{3, 1, 2\}$$$. Now let's walk through example interaction step by step.
First, you've made a query where $$$x=2$$$ and $$$m=2$$$. As you can see, $$$p[2]=1$$$, so our guess was wrong and you received an answer equal to $$$0$$$.
Secondly, you've made a query where $$$x=1$$$ and $$$m=2$$$. This time $$$p[1]=3$$$, so you received an answer equal to $$$2$$$.
As result of these two queries you now know that $$$p_1=3$$$ and $$$p_2=1$$$. But wait! We still have one more element in permutation!
Now you need to guess $$$p_3$$$. Let's make a query where $$$x=3$$$ and $$$m=1$$$. Because $$$p_3=2$$$, you'll get an answer $$$0$$$ as a result.
After three queries you successfully guessed permutation.
Note that while interacting with solver you cannot use scanf or similar functions because they may hang. You must use getchar instead. Also after sending every query described above flush the output buffer.
Also note that interactor used during contests has somewhat different behavior than official tester interactors. It doesn't mean anything bad, it just makes debugging easier. Official tester interactors have ability to detect illegal queries.
Please look at notes for both solutions below to understand how to handle hacks correctly.
Note
A permutation of size $$$n$$$ is such an array consisting of $$$n$$$ distinct integers from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ in arbitrary order. For example, $$${[2,3,1]}$$$ and $$${[4,3,1,2]}$$$ are permutations, but $$${[1,2,2]}$$$ and $$${[4,5]}$$$ are not.
Below is an example of a solution that works correctly with the first test from the statement.
Solution A
This solution uses randomization in order to obtain expected number of guesses equal to $$$O(n)$$. However it's possible to solve it deterministically.
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
const int max_n = 95;
int p[max_n];
void set_p(int _p[]) {
    copy(_p + 1, _p + max_n + 1, p);
}
bool check() {
    bool ok = true;
    vector<int> cnt(max_n + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= max_n; ++i)
        if (++cnt[p[i]] == 2)
            ok = false;
    return ok;
}
int main() {
    srand(time(NULL));
    cout << "? ";
    for (int i = 1; i <= max_n; ++i)
        cout << i << ' ';
    cout << endl;
    int ans;
    cin >> ans;
    if (!check()) {
        puts("-2");
        exit(0);
    }
    if (ans != -1 && ans != 0 && ans != 1 && ans != 2) {
        puts("-2");
        exit(0);
    }
    map<pair<int,int>,int> mp;
    auto getAns=[&](int x,int y){
        pair<int,int>p={x,y};
        assert(mp.find(p)==mp.end());
        printf("? %d\n",y);
        fflush(stdout);
        int res;
        scanf("%d",&res);
        mp[p]=res;
        return res;
    };
    vector<int>v;
    v.push_back(getAns(1,p[1]));
    for (int t = 2;;++t) {
        int l = lower_bound(v.begin(),v.end(),getAns(t,p[t])) - v.begin();
        int r = upper_bound(v.begin(),v.end(),getAns(t,p[t])) - v.begin();
        if (l==r) break;
        v.insert(v.begin()+max(l,r)-1,getAns(t,p[t]));
    }
    reverse(v.begin(),v.end());
    swap(p,v.back());
    sort(v.begin(),v.end(),
         [&](int i,int j){return mp[{i,i}]<mp[{j,j}];});
    for (auto &it : v)
        swap(it,p[it]);
    assert(check());
    puts("! ");
    for (int i = 1; i <= max_n; ++i)
        printf("%d ",p[i]);
    putchar('\n');
    fflush(stdout);
}
